    "summary": "Lucy Chase returns to her hometown of Plumpton (funny name I know), Texas, after becoming the centre of a murder investigation. Years earlier, she was found wandering along a road covered in blood, with no memory of what happened to her best friend Savvy. Now, when a true-crime podcast revisits the unsolved case, Lucy finds herself dragged back into the mystery. Everyone seems to have an opinion about what happened, but with a town full of secrets and a narrator who cannot always trust her own memories, uncovering the truth becomes anything but simple.",
    "reviewText": "I went into this book expecting a fairly conventional murder mystery, but Lucy Chase made it anything but ordinary. One of my favourite things about this read was definitely the humour. Despite dealing with a murder investigation and some genuinely unsettling events, the book manages to be surprisingly funny without taking away from the mystery itself. And honestly, Lucy might just be one of my favourite main characters ever. Her sarcastic inner commentary reminded me of myself more than once, and I loved getting little glimpses into the way her brain works. I found myself looking forward to her reactions just as much as the actual investigation, which is probably a good indication of how much I enjoyed her character.\n\nThe podcast format was another detail I really enjoyed. It added a completely different layer to the story because we weren't just following an investigation; we were also getting to see how that investigation was being presented to everyone else. The interviews, public speculation and different accounts of what happened made me realise how easily a story can change depending on who's telling it. Everyone seemed to have their own version of Lucy, and I found it interesting how quickly people were willing to believe certain things about her without really knowing what had happened. It also made the whole situation feel a lot more personal because Lucy wasn't just trying to figure out the truth herself; she was dealing with an entire town that had practically decided her story for her.\n\nThe pacing was great, and I found myself changing suspects constantly. Every time I thought I had finally figured something out, another little detail would make me question everything all over again. I especially liked how the book played around with memory and perception. Lucy's missing memories made it difficult to know what to believe, and I loved that even the smallest interactions could leave me wondering whether there was something more going on. It made me realise just how much we rely on our own memories when trying to understand situations, and how unsettling it would be to have everyone else remember something that you simply couldn't.\n\nIf I had one criticism, it would be that a few of the revelations felt slightly convenient, and I would have liked certain parts of the mystery to be explored a little further. Nevertheless, this was such an entertaining read. I loved the balance between humour and suspense, and I think Lucy is what really makes the book stand out. I would genuinely love to read another book featuring her because, at this point, I'm probably just as interested in following her thoughts as I am in solving the actual murder.",
