    "oneSentenceTakeaway": "A brilliant, feminist retelling of Medusa’s myth that questions who gets called a monster—and who gets remembered as a hero. ",
    "tagline": "A brilliant, feminist retelling of Medusa’s myth that questions who gets called a monster—and who gets remembered as a hero. ",
    "summary": "In Stone Blind, Natalie Haynes retells the myth of Medusa, a mortal girl raised by her immortal Gorgon sisters. After Poseidon assaults Medusa in Athene’s temple, Athene punishes Medusa by transforming her into a Gorgon whose gaze turns living beings to stone. Forced into isolation, Medusa’s story eventually collides with Perseus’s quest to claim a Gorgon’s head. Through shifting perspectives and sharp humour, the novel challenges the traditional portrayal of Medusa as a monster and Perseus as a hero.",
    "reviewText": "### Dissecting the Narrative • The Forensic Autopsy\n\nI chose *Stone Blind* because I already knew about Medusa’s assault and wanted to explore the story beyond the version of the myth I had grown up hearing. I knew she was transformed into a Gorgon, that Perseus eventually hunted her, and that her head became a weapon. What I wanted to understand was how the story had reached that point and, more importantly, what Medusa’s experience of it all had actually been.\n\nWhat I found particularly interesting was how Natalie Haynes uses different perspectives to reveal the injustice behind the myth. My obsession with true crime has made me increasingly interested in how stories are told, how evidence and perspectives shape public opinion, and how often victims are judged while those responsible escape the same scrutiny. I see so many real-world examples where the person who has been wronged ends up being questioned, blamed or forgotten. Seeing something similar unfold in Medusa’s story made it feel much more human and real to me. Through Medusa’s perspective, she is no longer just a terrifying figure; she is a young woman with sisters, relationships and a life that is taken away from her. Through Athene’s perspective, we see how Medusa is transformed and punished for Poseidon’s actions, while Poseidon himself does not face the same consequences. That contrast made the injustice even harder to ignore.\n\nThe shifting perspectives also changed how I viewed Perseus. I had always understood him as the hero of the myth, but Haynes makes us look beyond that familiar label and consider the story from the perspective of the person being hunted. We see how Medusa becomes a target in someone else's heroic journey, with her own fears and humanity pushed aside. It made me question how easily history turns one person into a hero and another into a monster, simply because of whose perspective gets remembered.\n\nBy the end, I felt that I had almost been introduced to an entirely different Medusa. I already knew what had happened to her, but I had never considered what it might have felt like to live through it. I found that especially difficult to separate from the injustices I see around me. Medusa deserved to be remembered for more than her appearance, her powers, or the fear she inspired. She deserved to have her story told as a person, not just as a monster. Stone Blind makes a meaningful effort to give Medusa that humanity, and I hope it inspires other readers to look beyond the familiar myth and research the stories and perspectives we may have overlooked.",
