    "tagline": "A gripping exploration of survival, resilience and identity that examines how people reclaim control over their lives after prolonged captivity and trauma.",
    "summary": "After an accident sidelines her Olympic ambitions, Miley takes a job at a remote mountain lodge in Idaho. However, a morning run turns into a nightmare when she is abducted by Fred and his son Hamish and taken to an isolated cabin. There, she encounters Mary, another woman held captive for years. As Miley struggles to escape, her friend Brent and lodge employee Wes begin searching for her, refusing to accept that she has simply disappeared. Through alternating perspectives, the novel explores survival, psychological control, trauma and the determination to reclaim one's identity.",
    "reviewText": "Honestly wasn't prepared for just how stressful this read would become. One of my favourite things about Gray After Dark was the setting. There's something particularly unsettling about the idea of being stranded in the middle of an enormous wilderness, completely cut off from the rest of the world. The cabin, the surrounding forest and the sheer distance from civilisation create an atmosphere that makes everything feel so much more threatening.\n\nMiley was definitely one of the strongest parts of the book for me. I loved how her background as an athlete actually became an important part of her survival rather than just being an interesting detail about her character. Her discipline, determination and ability to think strategically all come into play. What I found particularly interesting, though, was that her biggest struggle wasn't just physical. Her guilt surrounding her mother's death had clearly affected the way she viewed herself, and I liked how her personal journey developed alongside the survival storyline.\n\nThe dynamic between Miley and Mary was another part I really enjoyed. At first, I wasn't entirely sure what to make of Mary, and I found myself constantly questioning how much she knew and whether she could actually be trusted. Watching their relationship develop was particularly interesting because it added another emotional layer to a story that could otherwise have focused entirely on escaping the cabin. Their eventual alliance became one of the most important parts of the book.\n\nI also really appreciated the alternating perspectives. Being able to follow Miley's experience while also seeing Brent and Wes trying to find her created a completely different kind of tension. Every time the story switched away from Miley, I found myself wanting to get straight back to her chapters, but I also loved seeing the search unfold. Knowing that people were getting closer while Miley was still trapped made the whole situation feel even more urgent.\n\nIf I had one criticism, it would be that certain parts of the story felt quite drawn out, particularly as Miley's captivity continued. I understand why the author took the time to establish just how difficult her situation was, but there were moments when I was desperately waiting for something to change. Nevertheless, the payoff was worth it. This was such an intense read, and I think the combination of survival, psychological tension and character development made it stand out.",
